terms=['lumbar spine DXA region of interest placement reproducibility BMD','DXA lumbar spine analysis manual placement vertebral boundaries BMD','ISCD lumbar spine DXA ROI analysis vertebrae labeling precision']
for q in terms:
 r=requests.get('https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi',params={'db':'pubmed','term':q,'retmode':'json','retmax':8},timeout=20)
 ids=r.json().get('esearchresult',{}).get('idlist',[])
 print('\nQUERY',q,'PMIDs',ids)
 if ids:
  x=requests.get('https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esummary.fcgi',params={'db':'pubmed','id':','.join(ids),'retmode':'json'},timeout=20).json()['result']
  for i in ids:
   z=x[i]; print(i,z.get('pubdate'),z.get('title'))", 'language': 'python'}

The targeted PubMed queries also returned no direct lumbar-spine ROI paper, so I cannot substantiate a size or direction of change for an inconsistently filled “bottom” boundary. I’ll make one focused final literature search for clinical densitometry standards; a general methodological conclusion can still be made if guidance confirms that analysis boundaries define the measured vertebral region.

